First of all, I cannot see any team or designer designing a roguelike
without having played successful, existing roguelikes.  Nethack,
obviously, is one, I'd consider it probably the most successful.
There are elements of the game that will influence your ideas of
"roguelike-isms", and thus affect how you design your own roguelike,
whether or not the similarities are intentional.  I have no doubt that
Shiren, Izuna, Tao, and Mystery Dungeon were all influenced by
nethack, hack, or rogue.  In fact, I'd like to say that the main
reason for the large *differences* between Shiren (and Izuna, etc) and
nethack ( and hack, rogue, etc) is that they are catering to a modern
crowd.  The learning curve for nethack is steep.  It's almost a
requirement to simplify the system.  I think that the relationship is
that the oldschool roguelikes are being taken and funneled through
various themes and being made into acceptable games (by common market/
gamer standards).

I'd say the main reason nethack is the deepest / strongest entry in
the genre is because it is established (almost two decades old now, I
think?) and it is open source.  Its creation is the result of the
contribution of many, something that doesn't have a chance to happen
in game development companies today.

